---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import CalcMoneyArea from '@/components/tools/everyday/CalcMoneyArea.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="concrete-calculator">
  <CalcMoneyArea client:load variant="concrete" />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Volume, not area</h2>
    <p>
      For concrete, the third dimension matters. The concrete calculator uses these formulas:
    </p>
    <pre><code>slab:        length × width × (thickness ÷ 12)      [cubic feet]
round hole:  π × (diameter ÷ 2)² × (depth ÷ 12)     [cubic feet]
cubic yards: cubic feet ÷ 27
cubic metres: cubic feet × 0.0283168</code></pre>
    <p>
      A 20 × 12 ft patio slab 4 inches thick is 20 × 12 × 0.333 = 80 cubic feet, or 2.96 cubic yards.
      With a 10% allowance you order 3.26, which in practice is 3.5 yards, because trucks deliver in
      quarter-yard increments.
    </p>

    <h2>Bags or a truck?</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full min-w-[440px] border-collapse text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Bag</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">Yield</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">Bags per cubic yard</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">Bags per cubic metre</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="tabular-nums">
          <tr><td>40 lb</td><td style="text-align:right">0.30 cu ft</td><td style="text-align:right">90</td><td style="text-align:right">118</td></tr>
          <tr><td>50 lb</td><td style="text-align:right">0.375 cu ft</td><td style="text-align:right">72</td><td style="text-align:right">94</td></tr>
          <tr><td>60 lb</td><td style="text-align:right">0.45 cu ft</td><td style="text-align:right">60</td><td style="text-align:right">79</td></tr>
          <tr><td>80 lb</td><td style="text-align:right">0.60 cu ft</td><td style="text-align:right">45</td><td style="text-align:right">59</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The practical crossover is at about one cubic yard. Below that, bags are simpler and there is no
      truck minimum. Above it, you mix forty-five 80 lb bags by hand, which is about two tonnes of
      material and most of a day. A ready-mix truck is then cheaper and faster.
    </p>
    <p>
      Most suppliers have a minimum load (often 1 cubic yard) and a short-load surcharge below a full
      truck, so ask first. Trucks also have a limited discharge time before the mix starts to set. Have
      the forms, reinforcement and people ready before the truck arrives.
    </p>

    <h2>Typical thicknesses</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full min-w-[380px] border-collapse text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Application</th>
            <th style="text-align:right">Thickness</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="tabular-nums">
          <tr><td>Footpath, patio, shed base</td><td style="text-align:right">4 in / 100 mm</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Driveway (cars)</td><td style="text-align:right">4–5 in / 100–125 mm</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Driveway (heavy vehicles)</td><td style="text-align:right">6 in / 150 mm</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Garage floor</td><td style="text-align:right">5–6 in / 125–150 mm</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Fence post hole depth</td><td style="text-align:right">⅓ of post height, min 24 in</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="text-sm">
      Local building codes and frost-depth requirements override any rule of thumb, mostly for
      footings: in cold climates, footings must be below the frost line.
    </p>

    <h2>Post holes</h2>
    <p>
      Use the round column shape: enter the hole diameter and the depth in inches, and set the quantity
      to the number of identical holes. A 12 inch diameter hole 36 inches deep is 2.36 cubic feet, so six
      holes are 14.1 cubic feet, or twenty-four 80 lb bags.
    </p>
    <p>
      The post takes up some of that volume. A 4 × 4 in post in a 12 in hole fills about 11% of the
      volume, which the waste allowance covers. For a larger post in a tight hole, subtract its volume.
    </p>

    <h2>Why to over-order, not under-order</h2>
    <p>
      Running out during a pour is the one mistake in concrete work that you cannot fix later. The first
      pour starts to cure immediately, and more concrete added half an hour later makes a cold joint: a
      permanent plane of weakness and a visible line. Five to ten per cent extra is standard, for three
      reasons:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>The sub-base is never perfectly level, and low spots take more concrete than expected.</li>
      <li>Timber forms bend outwards under the weight of wet concrete.</li>
      <li>Spillage, and the remainder in the barrow and the mixer.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What the volume does not tell you</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Strength: the mix specification (3,000 psi / C20-25 and up) depends on the application and the
        climate, not the quantity.
      </li>
      <li>
        Reinforcement: rebar, mesh, fibre and control joints are separate decisions. The joints decide
        if the slab cracks in a straight line or randomly.
      </li>
      <li>
        Sub-base: most slabs need a compacted hardcore or gravel layer below, which is a separate volume
        calculation.
      </li>
      <li>
        Weight: concrete is about 150 lb per cubic foot, or 2,400 kg per cubic metre. A cubic yard is
        about two tonnes; know this before you plan the access.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Mixed jobs</h2>
    <p>
      Add a slab row for each flat pour and a round column row for each set of holes, and set quantities
      where they repeat. The total covers the whole job. Give that number to the supplier. It exports to
      CSV, Excel or PDF with the individual pours, so others can check the breakdown.
    </p>

    <h2>Works on site, with no signal</h2>
    <p>
      There is no server behind the page. When it has loaded, it runs on your device, so it works in a
      garden with no reception. Your list is saved only if you turn on saving, and only in this browser.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
